  "content": {
    "blocks": [
      {
        "kind": "text",
        "body": {
          "en": "Expanding, factorising, solving and rearranging — plus reading and sketching graphs. This is the strand that feeds directly into AS and A-Level, so the habits you build here matter well beyond the exam.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Definition 2.1 — Factorising a quadratic",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "To factorise \\( x^{2}+bx+c \\), find two numbers that <strong>multiply to \\( c \\)</strong> and <strong>add to \\( b \\)</strong>. They become the constants in \\( (x+p)(x+q) \\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Difference of squares",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( a^{2}-b^{2}=(a-b)(a+b) \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Quadratic formula",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( x=\\dfrac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Arithmetic nth term",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\( a+(n-1)d \\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Rearranging",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "apply inverse operations to <em>both</em> sides",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 2.A",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Solve the simultaneous equations \\( x+y=7 \\) and \\( 2x-y=2 \\).</p><p>Adding eliminates \\( y \\): \\( 3x=9 \\), so \\( x=3 \\). Substitute back into the first equation:</p>\\[ x=3,\\qquad y=4. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 2.B",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Find the \\( n \\)th term of \\( 5,\\,8,\\,11,\\,14,\\ldots \\)</p><p>The common difference is \\( 3 \\) and the term before the first would be \\( 2 \\):</p>\\[ u_n=3n+2. \\]",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Sequences &amp; nth Term",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "An <strong>arithmetic sequence</strong> increases/decreases by a fixed common difference \\(d\\). The \\(n\\)th term is \\(u_n = a+(n-1)d\\) where \\(a\\) is the first term. A <strong>geometric sequence</strong> multiplies by a common ratio \\(r\\): \\(u_n=ar^{n-1}\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "cards",
        "items": [
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Arithmetic \\(u_n\\)",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\(a+(n-1)d\\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Geometric \\(u_n\\)",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "\\(ar^{n-1}\\)",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Linear inequality",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "Flip sign when multiplying/dividing by negative",
              "ar": null
            }
          },
          {
            "label": {
              "en": "Graph types",
              "ar": null
            },
            "value": {
              "en": "linear, quadratic (∪∩), cubic (S), reciprocal (1/x)",
              "ar": null
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 2.C — nth term",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Find the \\(n\\)th term of: 5, 9, 13, 17, …</p><p>\\(d=4\\), first term \\(a=5\\). &nbsp; \\(u_n=5+(n-1)\\times4=4n+1\\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "def",
        "label": {
          "en": "● Linear Inequalities &amp; Graph Recognition <span class=\"tier-e\">E</span>",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "Solve like an equation but <strong>reverse the inequality sign</strong> when multiplying or dividing by a negative number. Graph types you must recognise: <em>linear</em> \\(y=mx+c\\) (straight line), <em>quadratic</em> \\(y=ax^2+bx+c\\) (parabola), <em>cubic</em> \\(y=ax^3+\\ldots\\) (S-shape), <em>reciprocal</em> \\(y=\\dfrac{k}{x}\\) (two branches), <em>exponential</em> \\(y=a^x\\) (crosses \\(y\\)-axis at 1).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "example",
        "label": {
          "en": "Worked example 2.D — Linear inequality",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p>Solve \\(3-2x&lt;11\\) and represent on a number line.</p>\\[-2x&lt;8\\;\\Rightarrow\\;x&gt;-4.\\]<p>Open circle at \\(-4\\), arrow pointing right.</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "The \\(n\\)th term of a sequence is \\(3n-7\\). (a) Find the 10th term. (b) Which term has value 23?",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "(a) \\(3(10)-7=23\\). (b) \\(3n-7=23\\Rightarrow3n=30\\Rightarrow n=10\\). Term 10 has value 23.",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "figure",
        "figure": {
          "type": "fn",
          "fn": "x^2 - 4*x + 3",
          "xrange": [-1, 5],
          "yrange": [-2, 6]
        },
        "caption": {
          "en": "Quadratic graph \\(y = x^2 - 4x + 3\\) intersecting the x-axis at roots \\(x = 1\\) and \\(x = 3\\), with axis of symmetry \\(x = 2\\) and vertex minimum at \\((2, -1)\\).",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "callout",
        "style": "warn",
        "label": {
          "en": "⚠️ Cambridge 0580 Traps — Algebraic Operations & Inequalities",
          "ar": null
        },
        "body": {
          "en": "<p><strong>Sign Flips in Inequalities:</strong> When dividing or multiplying by a negative number, the inequality sign MUST reverse: \\(-3x \\le 12 \\Rightarrow x \\ge -4\\). Forgetting this flip is the #1 lost mark in Cambridge algebra.</p><p><strong>Expanding Binomial Squares:</strong> Remember that \\((a - b)^2 = a^2 - 2ab + b^2\\), NOT \\(a^2 - b^2\\). Always write out \\((2x - 3)(2x - 3)\\) fully to avoid omitting the middle term \\(-12x\\).</p><p><strong>Quadratic Formula Denominator:</strong> The division line extends across the ENTIRE numerator: \\(x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\). Do not calculate \\(-b \\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{\\dots}}{2a}\\).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "practiceProblem",
        "question": {
          "en": "Solve the quadratic equation \\(2x^2 + 5x - 4 = 0\\). Give your solutions correct to 2 decimal places.",
          "ar": null
        },
        "solution": {
          "en": "<p>Identify coefficients: \\(a = 2\\), \\(b = 5\\), \\(c = -4\\).</p>\\[x = \\frac{-5 \\pm \\sqrt{5^2 - 4(2)(-4)}}{2(2)} = \\frac{-5 \\pm \\sqrt{25 + 32}}{4} = \\frac{-5 \\pm \\sqrt{57}}{4}\\]\\[x_1 = \\frac{-5 + 7.5498}{4} = \\frac{2.5498}{4} \\approx 0.64\\]\\[x_2 = \\frac{-5 - 7.5498}{4} = \\frac{-12.5498}{4} \\approx -3.14\\]<p>Solutions: \\(x = 0.64\\) or \\(x = -3.14\\) (both to 2 d.p.).</p>",
          "ar": null
        }
      }
    ]
  },
  "quizWidget": {
    "domains": ["Algebra & Graphs"],
    "enabled": true
  }
}
